@8kugames/dsh-zed-acp README · @8kugames/dsh-zed-acp：Built and tested against dsh `0.2.2-rc.1`. The plugin composes over the
installed harness — it ships no runtime of its own and never pins your key in
editor config.

@8kugames/dsh-zed-acp README · Install from a local clone：For development on the clone itself — tests, typecheck, rebuilds — run `npm install` once, then the usual `npm run typecheck` / `npm test` / `npm run build`.

Then point Zed at the profile (Zed → Settings → AI → External Agents, or
`settings.json`):

```jsonc
{
  "agent_servers": {
    "DeepSeek Harness": {
      "type": "custom",

@8kugames/dsh-zed-acp README · Install from a local clone：The first launch initializes `~/.dsh/profiles/zed`. Alternatively add the
plugin to the shipped automation profile — `dsh plugin --profile acp add
@8kugames/dsh-zed-acp` — the bundle patch disables the shipped
automation-only ACP transport so exactly one server owns stdio.

@8kugames/dsh-zed-acp README · Release：1. Bump `version` in `package.json` and `registry/agent.json` together.
2. Commit, then push the tag: `git tag zed-acp-v<version> && git push origin zed-acp-v<version>`.
3. The workflow `.github/workflows/zed-acp.yml` runs the three-platform test
   matrix on the tag and publishes to npm through Trusted Publishing (OIDC);
   no long-lived npm token is stored in the repository. It asserts that the tag
   matches both manifest versions.

